Date: Mon, 10 May 93 17:27:18 MDT
From: edlane@godot.Eng.Sandy.Novell.COM (Ed Lane)
Subject: Meta: Privatizing Moderators ( was:  Privatizing Judges)

I'm assuming my original post was lost due to a list outage, so here
we go again...
 
A (n indecent) proposal...

OK, yet another opinion on this thread.  I was initially of the opinion
that we should have more of an "NBA referee" model for settling
disputes on the list.  This allowed for near "real-time" temperature
(flame) control.  I now consider this impractical for various reasons.
Rather than having to mutually agree on some *adjudicator* we could
simply send our responses to approved list *moderators* after some
flame threshold had been exceeded (to be determined by the participants
or Harry).  These moderators could remove all the occurances of
"ignorance", "blind", "moronic", etc. from subsequent postings on a
burning thread.  All claims not substantiated in the posting (if not
obvious to the moderator) would be preceded with the "In my
opinion...."  "Me too's" could simply be dropped by the moderator.

It is important that the "moderations" made to a posting not be obvious
to list readers or the original "flame" will show through.  Ultimately this
allows for the discussion of emotional topics without the judgement
"RIGHT or WRONG".  This allows each of us to be our own jurist and get
enrichment from all sides of a controversy.

The word "moderator" is also much less threatening to the contributors
and would-be contributors of a thread.

A moderated thread could be prefaced with  "MOD:" so as to be obvious
to everyone reading and contemplating a response of their own.  This
informs them that they should seek a moderator for contributing. It is
felt (by me) that any attempt to build a software flame filter or other
elaborate schemes described on this list would be:

1) Defeated by we "technoids" on this list.  Heck, we would probably
consider it a challenge and an obligation to get a flame through once in
a while 8^).

2) Too cumbersome to use.

3) Abused. (I believe that this is happening now with the current system)

For this reason the "distributed-human-moderator-model" is recommended.

Comments?  Help, before an unacceptable decision is "hoisted" on us all.


-Ed
