From: carlf@ai.mit.edu (Carl Feynman)
Subject: Nanotech Beanstalks?

   From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu

      I read somewhere that diamond fiber (pure) is just good enough
   for a beanstalk to be built to orbit (from the ground). This is
   kind of depressing, because we are limited as to the strength of
   materials and our theoretical best barely gets us off the planet.
   Being an optimist, I hope that one day we can harness the strong
   nuclear force like we harness the electromagnetic force to build
   super strong materials.

The problem is that the only stable particles that feel the strong
force are protons and neutrons, and neutrons are only stable when
they're near protons.  Protons are positively charged, and neutrons
are uncharged.  So nuclear matter has a net positive charge, which
tends to blow it apart by electrostatic repulsion.  Now it's also
being held together by the stong force, so why can't that overcome the
repulsion?  The problem is that electrostatic repulsion is long range
(1/r^2) while strong-force attraction is short range (e^(-r)).  So in
a blob of nuclear matter, the blowing-up force is proportional to the
charge, which is proportional to the cube of the radius, while the
sticking-together force is proportional to the cross-sectional area,
which is proportional to the square of the radius.  A big enough
radius and the blob falls apart.  This is nuclear fission.

If there was a stable negatively charged strong-force-feeling
particle, we could make blobs of nuclear matter of unlimited size.  It
has been suggested that such blobs might exist, by incorporating
normally-unstable "strange" particles, forming blobs called
strangelets.  I don't want to go into the stabilization mechanism now
because I'm in a hurry; suffice it to say that it might or might not
work, and astrophysicists and experimentalists are combing the
universe for strangelets.

Nuclear matter is stabilized in neutron stars by compressing electrons
to a charge density equal to that of protons in the nuclear matter.
The negative charge of the electrons equals cancels the positive
charge of the protons, and there are no long-range forces to blow the
material apart.  This is called neutronium, because it is 98-99%
neutrons.  The protons form a crystal lattice of enormous stiffness.
Alas, this material cannot serve earthling engineers, for the
pressures required to compress the electrons are far higher than those
acheivable by ordinary materials.

If strangelets are possible, and we ever find or make one, we should
be able to breed unlimited quantities of strangelets from it.
(Warning: this is my own theory.  All previous paragraphs are
from peer-reviwed physics.)

     What about an active material, maybe a diamoid structure but strengthened
   somehow by an external mechanism (say, a strong magnetic force
   generated at the nanoscale between interlocking structures?)

Could be.  Big magnetic fields strengthen chemical bonds in the
direction of the field.  Unfortunately, concentrated magnetic fields
have an associated pressure which tends to blow them out perpendicular
to their field lines.  So such a structure might also require high
pressure.

Carbon is so weak and the universe is so big.  

Alas.

--carlf

