Perry says:
> Rich Walker says:
> > Curiously enough, there's a solution to this problem. ARM Ltd's
> > processor, a 32-bit RISC design, weighs in at a pitiful number of
> > transistors for its power, and so becomes an ideal vehicle for
> > testing far-out ideas: if you want to build nano-tech processors,
> > look at this one as a starting point. So simple is it's design (I
> > have no connection with them, except liking them) that a professor
> > at Manchester recently designed a fully-asynchronous version, which
> > I believe has been seen to work in producible silicon. This has two
> > advantages:
> > 
> >   1. No clock! No clock skew! No clock distribution!
> >   2. It's easy to port to faster technologies or slower ones.
> 
> Asynchronous digital circuit design is just asking for trouble.
> 
> 1. Have enormous fun constantly trying to solve metastability
>    problems. Higher the clock rate, the worse it gets. Any time you
>    need a synchronizer, you can't completely avoid the metastability
>    problem. Happy happy happy joy joy joy.
> 2. Have even more fun sweating over timing problems.

It depends on the style of asynchrounous design that you are practicing.  You
only have "synchronizers" if you have a free-running clock that defines the
timing of (some portion of) the system.  It's true that you can't get around
metastability, but you only have synchronizer failure when have to make a
decision in a fixed amount of time (in time for the next clock edge) and your
synchronizer doesn't make it.  On the other hand, you can use the same sort of
circuit as an "arbiter" -- to arbitrate between requests coming from multiple
async subsystems for instance -- and it will never "fail", you just can't make
any hard guarantees on how long it will take to decide.

As for your second point, the whole point of asynchronous design is to make the
timing problems easier.  If you properly define the signalling between modules,
and the composition rules for interconnecting them, then any legal
interconnection of modules will "make timing."  Which is to say, that it will
exhibit stable behavior at a rate determined by the slowest module.  And you've
eliminated the need for distributing a global clock to every point in the
system, which is becoming harder all the time as the clock rates go up and the
relative penalties of global and local signalling (such as off-chip versus
on-chip) get worse.  At my previous company, I was working on a high-perfomance
multiprocessor where we were trying to distribute a 7 nanosec clock to all parts
of the system with less than half a nanosec of skew worst case between clocked
elements.  What fun!

> The course recalled with the greatest fear and loathing of all as I
> contemplated my graduate corsework was asynchronous digital circuits.
> Pain and suffering beyond the capacity of humans to describe.

It really needn't be that bad.  Check out Ivan Sutherland's article about
"Micropipelines" in CACM a while back.  (I don't have the reference at my
fingertips, but I'll dig it up if there is interest.)

I agree that good old-fashioned asynchronous state machine design is truly
horrific.  You have a combinational logic network with some of the outputs fed
back to the inputs to keep track of the current state with no register/latch and
*no clock*.  Your problem, to design the network so that when the inputs change
it winds up in a stable state and makes only legal transitions.  Thanks, but no
thanks!

> I'm sure that async circuits that worked well could be designed -- by
> artificially intelligent computers working overtime. Mere humans are
> going to have to work with more reasonable methodologies for the
> forseeable future.

Not if you restrict the problem.  Note that the latches and flip-flops that we
all know and love are themselves async circuits "inside".  But we've bounded the
problem to an element with (essentially) two inputs, and we can make it work
subject to some constraints on the relative timing of the inputs (setup and hold
specs).  And once the latch/flop is done right, it makes the rest of the system
design much simpler.

Likewise, with the right set of primitives it is possible to make asyn design
tractable.  Signals between modules carry embedded information as to when they
are valid and when they aren't (instead of that information being carried by a
global clock.)  Arbitors allow you to merge signals analogous to a multiplexor.
And a module which accepts several signals and remembers the last state which
they had when they all indicated "valid" is the analog of a register.

Note that these techniques aren't in widespread use now.  As painful as it is,
it is still "cheaper" to distribute a clock.  But as chips get larger, the
switches smaller (and faster), and the relative communication delay between
widely separated parts of the chip grows, you can expect to see more and more
use of async techniques.

Sorry if this is too impenetrable, I'm in a hurry.  If anyone is interested, let
me know and I can try to make it clearer...

-chrism


