From: carlf@ai.mit.edu (Carl Feynman)
Subject: AI:Human Equivalence

   From: Michael Howard <michh@castle.edinburgh.ac.uk>

    When (roughly) will computer technology go beyond 'human equivalence'?
   specifically, considering previous rates of technological advance,
   roughly when will we have the necessary hardware so, if we had the
   right algorithm, the AI produced could surpass it's creator in the ability
   to create AI 

    Have heard guesses from books/tv mostly ranging from about 20-50 years
   'till 'human equivalence' in memory capacity, computational power, etc.,

I think the answer is about zero years.  More exactly, I think that if
you are willing to create an AI without sensory perception or motor
control, i.e. a thinking machine rather than a robot, you could run
something of humanlike abilities on a machine as powerful as a modern
supercomputer costing a few million dollars.

Why do I think this?  Well, the amount of information a human can
learn into long-term memory is limited to about 1 bit/second.  So
someone like me, who is about 10^9 seconds old, can't know more than
125 megabytes consciously.   Add a factor of 10 for unconsciously
known information (skills and expectations) and the total is only 1.25
Gbytes.  So the total memory capacity needed is well within the
abiliities of large modern computers.

The hard question is, how much processing per second is performed on
this information?  Estimates vary, and there isn't any good way to
settle this question.  If every neuron firing is laden with meaning,
the processing requirements are far beyond us.  However, if, as I
believe, the brain is not well designed for rational thought, the
processing required might well be only a few billion instructions per
second.  I'd be glad to elaborate on the reasons I believe this, if
anyone cares.

I was at a workshop last year concerned with this very question, and a
reasonable fraction of the participants agreed that the hardware for
human-level thought was within our grasp.  Actually, we agreed it was
in the next room, which contained a 1024-processor CM-5.

Now it's a mere problem of software. (:-)

