Date: Mon, 10 May 93 18:30:05 -0700
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: The Incredibly High Cost of High Tech

Perry Metzger comments on Marc Ringuette's remarks on my original figures:

>I think what is being missed here is the vast increases in the volume
>of chips being shipped. The $5 billion doesn't represent a rising cost
>for a given volume of chips -- it represents the anticipation of sales
>of a vastly larger number of chips. Its not unexpected that as
>production volume rises total factory costs might rise even as
>per-unit costs fall. Compare current yearly volume of computer sales
>to just a decade ago and you will see what I mean.

Perry is right: what I was presenting (the $100M-$1B-$5B sequunce for
386/468/586 (Pentium)) reflected increased volume as well as increased cost
to make each chip. Total volume for the 386 was several times that of the
286, total volume of the 486 will likely be 2-3 times that of the 386, and
Intel is _anticipating_ about a doubling for the Pentium. (I didn't write
down the numbers but I think they went something like this: 10 million 386s
in peak year, 30 million 486s in peak year, projected 60 million Pentiums
in peak year...don't ask me where Intel will find the customers! Caveat:
There's a _chance_ these numbers are total volumes....the slide was only on
the screen briefly and I wasn't taking notes).

Bear in mind that the total cost going into a chip is not just the capital
cost...salaries, consumables, etc. also enter in. Still, that the capital
costs are going up about 5x while volumes are only 2-3x, shows why Intel
would _like_ to get about 2-3x the average selling price of the 486. Should
enough competitors appear and the price competition accelerate, the Pentium
may not may much money for Intel.

This happens all the time in the chip industry--not all chips end up making
profits. Some chips are too big (the Pentium?), some just are too hard to
make (SuperSPARC?), and some just generate little interest (88100).

On my "predicition" of a $100 B plant cost, eventually (I think I estimated
this was many years off, around 2010 or so), this was just a speculation.
If a supplier can make chips more competitively with a very large plant
than a smaller, "less monopolistic" (to borrow Marc's phrasing), as has so
far been the case almost universally, then the larger plant will thrive and
the smaller plants will not.

("Small is beautiful" hasn't worked for auto plants, for example.)

A plausible scenario for a chip industry that is several hundred billion
dollars in size (total sales per year, for example): imagine one billion
people in the first world spending on average a few hundred dollars per
year for chips for computers, video, agents, communications, etc. Plus
another billion or so spending somewhat less. It adds up. (And of course it
may _not_ add up...lots of companies multiplied some large number of
"likely" customers by the sales price of their product and concluded they'd
be the next Microsoft!)

Cheaper Fab Equipment? As Nick Szabo can attest, I've done some speculative
work on what might be a bridge between conventional lithography (which is a
big part of the multibillion dollar cost of the new fabs) and
nanotechnology: the use of tip arrays to directly write wafers at 100
nanometer (0.1 micron, or about 4-6x better than anything now in
production) resolutions. But I don't really think this makes for "cheap
fabs," at least not for chips that are selling in the tens of millions per
year arena. And let me add a vast amount of work would be needed to build
such nanotech-related tools. (Another topic.)

If it'll make Marc feel any better, I'll slightly modify my prediction
(still a guess) and forecast the first $10 billion of "installed capacity"
by a single company (probably Intel, if the Pentium succeeds as
planned...otherwise, somebody else?) sometime around 1999-2002 and the
first $20 billion a few years later. Beyond that it's too murky to predict.

By the way, if this topic interests any of you Extropians, you should check
out Nick Tredennick's article (in Mike Slater's "Microprocessor Report,"
circa about a year ago...I don't have the issue, so don't ask me for more
details) on the incredible economies of scale the leading chip companies
have, especially as compared to the companies like NexGen and even Cyrix,
which will have a hard time recouping their development expenses.
Tredennick, a former 68000/68020 designer, has some amazing things to say
about this.

-Tim May

