Date: Mon, 10 May 1993 20:58:30 -0600
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: Meta: Privatizing Judges II

One more question for those of you interested in allowing folks to contract
around the list's legal services: should the List Administrator enforce
decisions of third-party adjudicators?  

Example:  Able and Betty agree to have Cal adjudicate their disputes.  They
then flame each other, and ask Cal to settle the issue.  Cal finds Able
guilty of starting the fight, and sentences him to neither receive messages
from nor send messages to the list for 10 days.

Question:  Should Cal be able to have the List Administrator enforce his
decision?  Or should Able, Betty, and Cal work out their own means of
enforcement?

My current favored answer: The List Administrator should be given the
discretion to enforce adjudication contracts between the list's members. 
This gives third-party adjudicators muscle that they would normally have in
realspace, but that can only be provided through the List Administrator
here.  And we want to make sure that third-party adjudicator's can
flourish.

Will this increase the List Administrator's burdens?  Not if he/she is
thereby freed from hearing those claims.  The List Administrator would
merely implement the adjudicator's decision.  

Worries about fraud will be largely overcome by simply having all concerned
parties send the List Administrator a message saying the adjudicator's
decision will be binding.  This should, of course, be sent prior the
adjudicator's decision.

Adjudicators would of course be free to issue decisions that can be
enforced without the List Administrator's help.  Example:  having one party
apologize to the other.

The List Administrator would have the discretion to not enforce any
adjudication that he/she judges to be contrary to list policies.  Example:
a adjudication that penalizes one party for advocating cryonics.

